[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning  saturday  25 years ago today one of the deadliest  aircraft disasters in american history  shook the world 230 people were on board  twa  flight 800 when the 747 jet bound from  new york to paris  exploded off the coast of long island  now the reconstructed wreckage is being  decommissioned and destroyed after being  carefully mapped and photographed over a  long period  errol barnett looks back at how this  disaster changed  aviation and so many lives forever  he was a harvard hockey player  and i was a new college graduate heidi  snow senator and her fiance michelle  bristoff had  one last phone call before his trip to  paris aboard twa  flight 800. we exchanged her final i  love views and he  told me he'd call me when he got to  paris we are about 6000 feet hovering  above the wreckage  but when her mother phoned telling her  to turn on the television  everything changed i saw the  tw 800 debris burning on the dark  atlantic ocean  and everything we had planned together  started to shatter  minutes after takeoff from jfk at almost  14  000 feet 230 passengers and crew from 10  countries  experienced a mid-air explosion the fuel  was  burning the water and you couldn't stand  the smell  or the fumes they were just burning your  eyes  one of the first reporters on the scene  cbs news senior investigative producer  pat  milton who at the time was with the  associated press  i remember there was one young man and  here he was  hauling bodies into the boat but  treating them with such  dignity and grace it was just an  incredible incredible experience to see  there were no survivors public outcry  was immediate  and suspecting a crime the fbi launched  an exhaustive investigation  they thought that this was a terrorist  act  it captured the world trawlers vacuuming  the ocean floor  sifting through sediment for clues what  followed was one of the longest fbi  investigations of its time  and one of the longest ntsb probes to  this day  this is where the the main explosion  initiated  this is the area of interest frank  hildrop who was among the first  ntsb investigators on site says  reconstructing the doomed aircraft  a rare move for the safety board was  essential we wanted to be able to  view and display the  the center wing tank to demonstrate  where the event occurred  and how the spark may have been  introduced to it it took 16 months for  the fbi to rule out terrorism and more  than four years and 40 million dollars  for the ntsb to conclude  the explosion was most likely caused by  a short circuit  outside of the center wing tank this  changed the way planes were built  specifically how fuel tanks were  constructed  a tragedy has resulted  in a number of significant improvements  both in aviation as well as in  victim assistance family assistants the  wreckage has been off limits to cameras  sitting in this virginia ntsb  facility since 2003 a sacred teaching  tool for crash investigators  but with the lease here expiring this  wreckage is being destroyed  and won't go to a museum due to family  wishes  it's lessons moving online can a virtual  environment  ever really replace being here in real  life i think that's the question we're  all asking ourselves  as we've gone through the pandemic our  ability to reach others  i think increases significantly by doing  it this way  and and i won't i won't disagree i i do  think we  may lose a little something in that  process  senator who is now married with children  spent the last 25 years running her  bereavement non-profit  called access now one of her daughters  is helping  other kids i have an older daughter who  actually has come forward and started  make her own initiative to help children  by putting together a project called  comfort kits for kids  where she actually puts together grief  kits and toys and  comforting objects to send to kids who  are in grief  what is the legacy of twa flight 800  it's the preciousness of life the fact  that you did have  ordinary people doing extraordinary  things  to not only try to recover victims but  try to  solve what happened to this plane crash  the investigators were driven  to find out and they were driven because  of their relationships with the families  the cbs this morning saturday errol  barnett washington  one of these mysteries that has just  endured  and it's one of those uh situations  where so many of the people who died on  that flight were from  the same community out in long island it  was  it's just hit them so hard i remember  covering one of the anniversaries and  it's just  it's it's just heartbreaking i mean and  when we see those those are those  mileposts that you say i remember where  i was i remember when that happened and  the fear then  was the thought that it was a bomb or  something like that and then there were  no answers for so long  and the good things to come out of this  horror what the kids have done and also  the way that planes have changed  learn from the tragedy as they talk to  them always

